1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,120
 Hey everyone, it's Dr. Marcon and this is chapter 5 on the integumentary system.

2
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:14,840
 So one of the organs in our integumentary system is the skin.

3
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:17,600
 So we have the skin and the hypodermis.

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:22,960
 The skin is our largest organ and it counts for about 7% of our body weight.

5
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:28,760
 It can vary in thickness from anywhere between 1.5 to 4.4 millimeters.

6
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:31,540
 It's divided into two very distinct layers.

7
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:36,400
 We have the outermost or more superficial layer which is the epidermis and then we have

8
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:37,840
 the dermis.

9
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:41,040
 Now the hypodermis lies deep to the dermis.

10
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:44,680
 It is composed of ariolar and adipose tissues.

11
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:52,120
 Now the hypodermis is not part of the integumentary system but does share some of the skin's properties.

12
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:56,880
 So here we see a slide of structure of the skin.

13
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:58,400
 You can see the very distinct layers.

14
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:04,620
 So here is the more superficial layer which is the epidermis and then we have the dermis

15
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:06,120
 which is deep to the epidermis.

16
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:09,340
 And we can see within the dermis there are two sublayers.

17
00:01:09,340 --> 00:01:14,160
 We have the papillary dermis and the reticular dermis.

18
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:19,200
 Also within this slide we can see the hypodermis which is below the dermis which is again

19
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:23,160
 composed of connective tissue as well as adipose tissue.

20
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:28,920
 Then we can see some nervous structures, sensory nerve fibers, the free endings.

21
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:34,480
 We can see lamellar corpuscles, we can see hair follicle receptors.

22
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:37,280
 And then we can see some of the appendages of the skin.

23
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:43,040
 We can see sweat glands, acrynslectric glands, erector p-line muscles, we can see oil glands

24
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:44,800
 or sebaceous glands.

25
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:51,200
 And then we see a hair follicle along with the hair root because we know that we do have

26
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:54,640
 hairs within our skin.

27
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:59,720
 So this is just a gross depiction of the skin and underlying tissues.

28
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:05,840
 So the more superficial layer again is the epidermis and below that is the dermis.

29
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:09,720
 Then we have the hypodermis again made up of that connective tissue as well as adipose

30
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:10,720
 tissue.

31
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:18,920
 We have some deep fascia and then deeper to that we have muscle.

32
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:22,320
 Now we have five very important functions to the skin.

33
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:25,640
 So first of all we have protection.

34
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:31,160
 The skin provides protection because it cushions organs and protects from bumps, chemicals,

35
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:35,200
 water loss and ultraviolet or UV radiation.

36
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:37,560
 Also it helps with body temperature regulation.

37
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:44,520
 We have our capillary network and sweat glands that help regulate heat loss.

38
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:50,440
 It also functions with excretion so we lose urea, salts and water that are lost through

39
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:51,440
 sweat.

40
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:57,120
 Also it helps with the production of vitamin D. We have epidermal cells that will use ultraviolet

41
00:02:57,120 --> 00:02:59,960
 radiation to synthesize vitamin D.

42
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:02,520
 And then we have sensory reception.

43
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:10,640
 Within the skin we have sense organs that are associated with nerve endings.

44
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:12,960
 So the first layer of the skin is the epidermis.

45
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:17,280
 The epidermis is composed of keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.

46
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:22,360
 Again we talked about the keratin being that protein that helps with waterproofing properties

47
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:23,880
 of our skin.

48
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:26,800
 So there are four main cell types within the epidermis.

49
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:28,880
 We have the keratinocytes.

50
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:31,440
 These are located in the stratum spinosum.

51
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:36,160
 They produce keratin, that fibrous protein that we talked about earlier when we were talking

52
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:37,600
 about epithelium.

53
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:39,560
 We also have melanocytes.

54
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:47,560
 They're located in the basal layer and they help manufacture and secrete the pigment melanin.

55
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:49,780
 We also have tactile epithelial cells.

56
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:52,760
 These are also located in the basal layer.

57
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:55,560
 These are attached to our sensory nerve endings.

58
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:57,720
 And then we have dendritic cells.

59
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:01,920
 These are found in the stratum spinosum and are part of the immune system and they act

60
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:02,920
 like macrophages.

61
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:11,020
 Again those pac-man cells that kind of engulf any foreign tissue or foreign bodies.

62
00:04:11,020 --> 00:04:13,000
 First we're going to talk about keratinocytes.

63
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,240
 These are the most abundant cell type in the epidermis.

64
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:20,960
 They come from the deepest layer of the epidermis and produce keratin.

65
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:27,800
 Keratin is a very tough fibrous protein, helps with the waterproofing properties of the skin.

66
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:31,120
 Keratinocytes also produce antibodies and enzymes.

67
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:34,940
 Now once they reach the skin surface, they die.

68
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:41,360
 So keratinocytes at the skin surface are dead, they're dead gem.

69
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:45,320
 So here are the layers of the epidermis.

70
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:55,400
 So the deepest layer, we have the stratum basale, also known as the stratum germanotovum.

71
00:04:55,400 --> 00:05:02,400
 And then above that we have the stratum spinosum then stratum granulosum.

72
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:05,480
 Stratum lucetum is only found in thick skin.

73
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:12,000
 So thick skin being the skin on the palms of our hands and the soles are our feet.

74
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,400
 And the most superficial layer is the stratum corneum.

75
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:21,400
 So a pneumatic device that I like to use, I'm actually a really huge Star Trek fan.

76
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:31,540
 So if you know Star Trek, there is actually a Star Trek with the character named Data.

77
00:05:31,540 --> 00:05:34,920
 And one of my favorite captains is Captain Picard.

78
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:36,800
 This is the next generation.

79
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:40,000
 So a good pneumatic that I like to use is BS.

80
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,960
 So Brent Spiner gained lieutenant command.

81
00:05:43,960 --> 00:05:46,720
 Brent Spiner is the actor that plays data.

82
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:54,520
 So Brent Spiner gained lieutenant command, helps you remember the layers of the epidermis

83
00:05:54,520 --> 00:05:57,240
 from the deepest layer to the more superficial layer.

84
00:05:57,240 --> 00:06:03,720
 Now mind you, this may become a bonus question, so I definitely study this very well.

85
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:11,840
 So Brent Spiner, so B stands for stratum basale, the deepest layer, S spiner stratum spinosum,

86
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:18,820
 which is above the stratum basale, then we have stratum granulosum gained, and then stratum

87
00:06:18,820 --> 00:06:24,680
 leucetum, which is only found in thick skin, lieutenant, and then stratum corneum command,

88
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:27,080
 which is the most superficial layer.

89
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:33,920
 So Brent Spiner gained lieutenant commander, a good pneumatic if you want to remember the

90
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:38,320
 different layers of the epidermis from deep to superficial.

91
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:43,920
 Now know that one of the bonus questions might ask you, name the layers from superficial

92
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:50,360
 to deep, so make sure you pay attention to what the bonus question is asking you.

93
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:55,320
 So here we see a slide of the different layers of the epidermis.

94
00:06:55,320 --> 00:07:02,080
 So below the epidermis we have the dermal layer, which contains, you can see here, our

95
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:04,360
 dense irregular connective tissue.

96
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:10,680
 And then we have the stratum basale, and the stratum basale is the deepest epidermal layer.

97
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:15,680
 It's one row of actively mitotic stem cells.

98
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:19,400
 Some newly formed cells become part of the more superficial layers.

99
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:26,400
 And then occasionally you can see melanocytes as well as dendritic cells.

100
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:32,520
 And then above the stratum basale, you will see the stratum spinosum.

101
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:35,620
 This is made up of several layers of keratinocytes.

102
00:07:35,620 --> 00:07:38,080
 They're joined by desmosomes.

103
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:43,360
 We talked about those structures that kind of join those cells together.

104
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:47,560
 Cells contain thick bundles of intermediate filaments made of pre-caratin.

105
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:52,600
 So this is before keratin is formed.

106
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:54,200
 We have pre-caratin.

107
00:07:54,200 --> 00:07:56,800
 Then above the spinosum we have stratum granulosum.

108
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:59,680
 This is one to five layers of flattened cells.

109
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:03,640
 At this point, the organelles have started deteriorating.

110
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:09,240
 The cytoplasm is full of laminar granules that release lipids.

111
00:08:09,240 --> 00:08:12,040
 And we have keratopiline granules.

112
00:08:12,040 --> 00:08:17,480
 And finally we have the most superficial layer that is the stratum corneum.

113
00:08:17,480 --> 00:08:23,600
 It's made up of about 23 layers of dead cells represented only by flat, membranous sacs filled

114
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:25,360
 with keratin.

115
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:30,320
 And within these cells we have glycol lipids in the extracellular space.

116
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:35,720
 So the more superficial layer is basically the dead layer that is the stratum corneum.

117
00:08:35,720 --> 00:08:38,760
 Now not represented in this slide is the stratum leucetum.

118
00:08:38,760 --> 00:08:42,680
 The stratum leucetum is actually only found in very thick skin.

119
00:08:42,680 --> 00:08:49,400
 Again, the skin of the palms of the hands and the soles of the feet.

120
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:55,280
 So here is a drawing, a figure of the different layers of the epidermis.

121
00:08:55,280 --> 00:09:03,200
 Again, you'll find different cells within the stratum base alleys such as melanocytes.

122
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:05,200
 We'll talk about melanocytes in just a little bit.

123
00:09:05,200 --> 00:09:08,360
 They secrete a specific pigment called melanin.

124
00:09:08,360 --> 00:09:16,840
 Melanin is basically what gives us the coloring in our skin, some more darker than others.

125
00:09:16,840 --> 00:09:22,840
 The stratum spinosum, again several layers of keratinocytes.

126
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:28,640
 And then the stratum granulosum, these, this layer contain cells whose organelles have

127
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:31,400
 started deteriorating, started dying off.

128
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:35,020
 And then finally we have the more superficial layer, the stratum corneum that contain dead

129
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:39,640
 cells and are filled with that protein keratin.

130
00:09:39,640 --> 00:09:46,520
 So we're going to talk about the different layers of the epidermis first.

131
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:49,380
 We're going to talk about the stratum base alley.

132
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:52,080
 The stratum base alley is the deepest layer of the epidermis.

133
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:56,480
 It is attached to the underlying dermis.

134
00:09:56,480 --> 00:10:02,680
 Cells here actively divide in order to kind of replace the more superficial cells.

135
00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:06,320
 We know that stratum base alley contains specific cells.

136
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:10,920
 For example, they have tactile epithelial cells that are associated with sensory nerve

137
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:11,920
 endings.

138
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:16,000
 Also, again, they contain those melanocytes that we talked about that secrete the pigment

139
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:23,400
 melanin that gives us our skin color.

140
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:30,520
 And then here we see our stratum base alley that the deepest layer of the epidermis.

141
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:33,880
 And just below the stratum base alley, we have our dermal layer.

142
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:39,520
 Again, our dermis is made up of dense irregular connective tissue.

143
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:41,680
 And there are different layers to the dermis.

144
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:45,440
 We have the papillary dermis and the reticular dermis.

145
00:10:45,440 --> 00:10:51,280
 We see that the papillary dermis looks like it has these finger-like projections that

146
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:53,800
 are going into the epidermis.

147
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:57,440
 So if you see sort of these fingers, these are papilla.

148
00:10:57,440 --> 00:10:58,840
 These are the dermal papilla.

149
00:10:58,840 --> 00:11:03,040
 And these papilla make up the papillary dermis.

150
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:07,160
 And then below the papillary dermis, we have the reticular dermis.

151
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:09,080
 And we'll get into the dermis in just a little bit.

152
00:11:09,080 --> 00:11:13,000
 But on this slide, you kind of want to focus on the different layers of the epidermis,

153
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,440
 especially in thick skin.

154
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:20,600
 We have our stratum base alley here that have mitotically active cells.

155
00:11:20,600 --> 00:11:26,980
 We then have the stratum spinosum above the base alley, the granulosum above the spinosum.

156
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:32,920
 And then finally, we have this sort of clear layer above the granulosum.

157
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:35,440
 And that is the stratum lucetum.

158
00:11:35,440 --> 00:11:39,040
 You'll only see the stratum lucetum again in thick skin.

159
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:42,200
 And then we have this very thick layer, the stratum corneum.

160
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:51,640
 Again, these are dead cells that have a high amount of the protein keratin.

161
00:11:51,640 --> 00:11:53,300
 Next layer is the stratum spinosum.

162
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:55,360
 It's the spiny layer.

163
00:11:55,360 --> 00:12:00,600
 The spiny appearance is caused by artifacts of histological preparation.

164
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:05,560
 It contains thick bundles of intermediate filaments that resist tension and contain the protein

165
00:12:05,560 --> 00:12:06,560
 precaritin.

166
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:10,600
 So this is precaritin before a keratin is formed.

167
00:12:10,600 --> 00:12:18,880
 Among the keratinocytes, our star-shaped dendritic cells are a type of macrophage and help function

168
00:12:18,880 --> 00:12:19,880
 in the immune system.

169
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:26,560
 Again, they act like Pacman cells to kind of engulf any foreign debris or any pathogens

170
00:12:26,560 --> 00:12:32,280
 that might be found in the epidermis.

171
00:12:32,280 --> 00:12:34,560
 The next layer is the stratum granulosum.

172
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:37,560
 Consists of a few layers of keratinocytes.

173
00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:43,320
 These keratinocytes will contain keratohilin granulins that help form keratin.

174
00:12:43,320 --> 00:12:50,240
 So from the precaritin, you'll need these granules to help form mature keratin or keratin

175
00:12:50,240 --> 00:12:51,800
 proteins.

176
00:12:51,800 --> 00:12:56,840
 Also there are laminar granules that contain the waterproofing glycolipid.

177
00:12:56,840 --> 00:13:03,200
 We know that granulosum above the stratum granulosum cells are too far from durable capillaries

178
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:04,560
 to receive nourishment.

179
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:11,880
 So the dermal capillaries, again, the epidermis is a vascular, but it receives nutrients from

180
00:13:11,880 --> 00:13:14,080
 the underlying dermis.

181
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:20,120
 But the stratum granulosum is a little too far away from the dermal capillaries, so it's

182
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:27,520
 not receiving the proper amount of nourishment and blood supply.

183
00:13:27,520 --> 00:13:29,800
 The stratum leucetum is a clear layer.

184
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:32,120
 It occurs only in thick skin.

185
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:36,960
 Again, you'll only find it in the palms of the hand and soles of the feet.

186
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:43,320
 It's composed of a few rows of flat dead keratinocytes.

187
00:13:43,320 --> 00:13:49,920
 And then above leucetum, we'll find the stratum corneum, the horny layer.

188
00:13:49,920 --> 00:13:55,520
 It is composed of a thick layer of dead keratinocytes and thickened plasma membranes.

189
00:13:55,520 --> 00:13:56,960
 This is the most superficial layer.

190
00:13:56,960 --> 00:14:02,960
 It protects the skin against abrasion and penetration.

191
00:14:02,960 --> 00:14:09,960
 So here we see in detail the epidermal skin structure.

192
00:14:09,960 --> 00:14:14,160
 So here is another pneumatic that will help you remember the names of the different layers

193
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:15,760
 in the epidermis.

194
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:18,720
 So basically the spinning by granny is loose and corny.

195
00:14:18,720 --> 00:14:20,240
 For me, I'm a Star Trek fan.

196
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:27,640
 So I like the pneumatic Brent Spiner Gains Lieutenant Commander, meaning he was promoted.

197
00:14:27,640 --> 00:14:32,960
 So whatever pneumatic you want to use, it'll help you remember the different layers within

198
00:14:32,960 --> 00:14:33,960
 the epidermis.

199
00:14:33,960 --> 00:14:47,960
 You can see a histological picture compared to a drawing or a figure representation.

200
00:14:47,960 --> 00:14:51,240
 So beneath the epidermis, we have the dermis.

201
00:14:51,240 --> 00:14:54,320
 The dermis is the second major region of the skin.

202
00:14:54,320 --> 00:14:57,680
 It is strong and flexible and has connected tissue.

203
00:14:57,680 --> 00:15:02,480
 It is richly supplied with blood vessels and nerves and has two very distinct layers within

204
00:15:02,480 --> 00:15:03,480
 the dermis.

205
00:15:03,480 --> 00:15:06,800
 We have the papillary dermis, which includes dermal papillary.

206
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:12,440
 Again, these are finger-like projections coming from the dermis that kind of project into

207
00:15:12,440 --> 00:15:14,040
 the epidermis.

208
00:15:14,040 --> 00:15:19,680
 And then we have a reticular dermis, which is a deeper layer and makes up about 80% of

209
00:15:19,680 --> 00:15:22,160
 thickness of the dermis.

210
00:15:22,160 --> 00:15:27,240
 So the papillary dermis includes structures called the dermal papillae.

211
00:15:27,240 --> 00:15:32,400
 They help increase surface area for exchange of gases, nutrients, and waste.

212
00:15:32,400 --> 00:15:35,480
 Dermal papillae lie on dermal ridges.

213
00:15:35,480 --> 00:15:41,320
 The epidermal ridges or friction ridges are elevations of the dermal ridges.

214
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:43,960
 These epidermal ridges are fingerprints.

215
00:15:43,960 --> 00:15:51,360
 They also increase the gripping ability of hands and feet.

216
00:15:51,360 --> 00:15:54,920
 So here is a picture of the epidermis and the dermis.

217
00:15:54,920 --> 00:16:02,720
 So within the dermis below our stratum, stratum-based alley, we have these finger-like projections

218
00:16:02,720 --> 00:16:04,360
 going into the epidermis.

219
00:16:04,360 --> 00:16:06,000
 This is our papillary layer.

220
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:13,200
 So the papillary layer is the layer directly below the deepest layer of the epidermis, the

221
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:14,320
 stratum-based alley.

222
00:16:14,320 --> 00:16:17,560
 So this layer, here is the papillary layer.

223
00:16:17,560 --> 00:16:22,680
 And underneath the papillary layer is the reticular layer, which makes up about 80% of

224
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:23,920
 the dermis.

225
00:16:23,920 --> 00:16:28,560
 And this next picture shows you a better picture of the different layers of the dermis.

226
00:16:28,560 --> 00:16:33,320
 So again, you have these papilla, or dermal papilla, that make up the papillary layer

227
00:16:33,320 --> 00:16:35,000
 of the dermis.

228
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:41,800
 And then you have this reticular layer just deep to the papillary layer.

229
00:16:41,800 --> 00:16:46,680
 And then we can see, you know, our dense irregular connective tissue, making up the different

230
00:16:46,680 --> 00:16:49,520
 layers of our dermis.

231
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:52,880
 So the reticular dermis accounts for 80% of dermal thickness.

232
00:16:52,880 --> 00:16:56,400
 Again, made up of dense irregular connective tissue.

233
00:16:56,400 --> 00:17:01,160
 We have cleavage lines that are separations between collagen fibers.

234
00:17:01,160 --> 00:17:06,040
 And the collagen fibers, we know, give skin its strength.

235
00:17:06,040 --> 00:17:14,520
 Dermal lines are deep creases in palms, wrists, soles, fingers, and toes.

236
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:20,280
 We know that the reticular dermis has a very rich nerve supply, has two vascular plexuses.

237
00:17:20,280 --> 00:17:24,280
 We have a dermal plexus, any sub-papillary plexus.

238
00:17:24,280 --> 00:17:31,120
 Blood vessels play a very important role in temperature regulation.

239
00:17:31,120 --> 00:17:36,480
 So here we can see the different ridges within our epidermis.

240
00:17:36,480 --> 00:17:42,280
 We know the epidermal ridges are just basically an elevation of the dermal ridges.

241
00:17:42,280 --> 00:17:46,160
 And we can see our flexure lines in our fingers, as well as in our palm.

242
00:17:46,160 --> 00:17:51,880
 If you ever go to a palm reader, these are the lines that they end up reading.

243
00:17:51,880 --> 00:17:57,600
 And then we do have cleavage lines in the reticular dermis.

244
00:17:57,600 --> 00:18:00,560
 So below the dermis, we have a layer called the hypodermis.

245
00:18:00,560 --> 00:18:05,520
 The hypodermis is not part of the skin, but it's a layer that is deep to the skin, and

246
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:08,480
 it's also called superficial fascia.

247
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:13,640
 We know that the hypodermis contains ariolar and adipose connective tissue.

248
00:18:13,640 --> 00:18:17,560
 The hypodermis is important because it anchors the skin to underlying structures and helps

249
00:18:17,560 --> 00:18:19,400
 insulate the body.

250
00:18:19,400 --> 00:18:25,280
 And we have different distributions of the hypodermis in males and females.

251
00:18:25,280 --> 00:18:27,200
 So how do we get our skin color?

252
00:18:27,200 --> 00:18:30,000
 We have three pigments that contribute to skin color.

253
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:31,000
 We have melanin.

254
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:34,860
 Melanin is the most important pigment made from tyrosine.

255
00:18:34,860 --> 00:18:40,040
 We know we have melanocytes that are located within the epidermis that produce melanin

256
00:18:40,040 --> 00:18:43,440
 and help contribute to the pigmentation of our skin.

257
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:44,440
 Carotene.

258
00:18:44,440 --> 00:18:49,760
 Carotene is a yellowish pigment from carrots and tomatoes, and we have hemoglobin.

259
00:18:49,760 --> 00:18:54,680
 We talked about hemoglobin being that protein important for our oxygen transport with our

260
00:18:54,680 --> 00:18:56,480
 red blood cells.

261
00:18:56,480 --> 00:19:03,440
 Caucasian skin contains very little melanin because we don't, caucasians don't have that

262
00:19:03,440 --> 00:19:07,240
 pigment to make their skin darker.

263
00:19:07,240 --> 00:19:12,680
 This allows the crimson color of the blood to kind of show through the pale skin.

264
00:19:12,680 --> 00:19:18,280
 So that's why most caucasians have that pinkish color towards that skin, to their skin because

265
00:19:18,280 --> 00:19:23,760
 of the protein hemoglobin.

266
00:19:23,760 --> 00:19:27,120
 Along with the integumentary system, we have skin appendages.

267
00:19:27,120 --> 00:19:32,140
 Skin appendages are basically derivatives of the epidermis, and this includes hair and

268
00:19:32,140 --> 00:19:38,920
 hair follicles, nails, sweat glands, and sebaceous glands.

269
00:19:38,920 --> 00:19:41,600
 These help maintain body homeostasis.

270
00:19:41,600 --> 00:19:45,160
 So first, we're going to talk about nails.

271
00:19:45,160 --> 00:19:49,280
 Nails are basically a scale-like modification of the epidermis.

272
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:53,760
 They're made up of very hard keratin, and we have different parts to nail.

273
00:19:53,760 --> 00:19:55,840
 We have the free edge.

274
00:19:55,840 --> 00:19:57,320
 There's the body.

275
00:19:57,320 --> 00:19:59,880
 There's the root of the nail.

276
00:19:59,880 --> 00:20:05,960
 There are the nail folds, and then we have the epinecium.

277
00:20:05,960 --> 00:20:11,600
 The epinecium is also known as the cuticle, and this is the part of the nail that we get

278
00:20:11,600 --> 00:20:17,160
 trimmed when we have a pedicura done, which is something I probably need to have done because

279
00:20:17,160 --> 00:20:21,560
 I haven't had one in a very long time.

280
00:20:21,560 --> 00:20:24,720
 So here we see the different structures of the nail.

281
00:20:24,720 --> 00:20:27,640
 Here is that free edge.

282
00:20:27,640 --> 00:20:32,960
 We have the nail plates, and then we have the epinecium, which is the cuticle.

283
00:20:32,960 --> 00:20:35,800
 This structure right here is the lunula.

284
00:20:35,800 --> 00:20:41,760
 It's sort of a half-moon-like structure that we see at the base of the nail plates, and

285
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:46,240
 we can see that the nail bed is actually embedded within the fingers.

286
00:20:46,240 --> 00:20:53,800
 We have folds that kind of go over the nail plates, and then we see the tip of the finger,

287
00:20:53,800 --> 00:21:01,000
 the finger tip, the distal phalanx that makes up that fingertip.

288
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:04,040
 Another appendage of the skin we're going to talk about is hair.

289
00:21:04,040 --> 00:21:10,720
 Hair are flexible strands of dead keratinized cells made up of hard keratin, which are tough

290
00:21:10,720 --> 00:21:13,480
 and durable, and there are chief parts of a hair.

291
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:18,360
 We have the root of the hair, which is embedded in the skin, and then we have the shaft.

292
00:21:18,360 --> 00:21:24,360
 The shaft of the hair is the part of the hair that projects above the skin's surface.

293
00:21:24,360 --> 00:21:29,280
 Hair are flexible strands that are produced by hair follicles and consists of dead keratinized

294
00:21:29,280 --> 00:21:30,280
 cells.

295
00:21:30,280 --> 00:21:34,840
 So the hair that you see on your head that you hold in your hand, this is actually dead

296
00:21:34,840 --> 00:21:35,840
 keratinized cells.

297
00:21:35,840 --> 00:21:39,540
 Again, we have different regions of the hair.

298
00:21:39,540 --> 00:21:41,880
 We have the shaft of the hair.

299
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:48,040
 This is where keratinization is complete, it consists of the dead keratinized cells, and

300
00:21:48,040 --> 00:21:52,840
 then we have the root of the hair that's embedded in the skin where keratinization is

301
00:21:52,840 --> 00:21:54,960
 still ongoing.

302
00:21:54,960 --> 00:21:59,760
 Hair follicles will fold down from the epidermal surface into the dermis, and then we have

303
00:21:59,760 --> 00:22:04,760
 specialized smooth muscles called erector pealae muscles.

304
00:22:04,760 --> 00:22:10,400
 Erector pealae muscles are bundles of smooth muscle cells that help pull the hair upright

305
00:22:10,400 --> 00:22:17,160
 in response to cold temperatures, or sometimes in response to fear, like an upcoming exam

306
00:22:17,160 --> 00:22:22,440
 or an upcoming quiz that Dr. Markon might be giving you or a surprise exam that Dr. Markon

307
00:22:22,440 --> 00:22:27,400
 might be giving you, which she won't because she wouldn't do that, or if there's a ghost

308
00:22:27,400 --> 00:22:33,440
 in the room or something scary on TV, again, these are erector pealae muscles, erector pealae

309
00:22:33,440 --> 00:22:42,720
 muscles respond to that and help bring the hair standing up and give you those goose pimples.

310
00:22:42,720 --> 00:22:45,280
 Hair has three concentric layers of keratinized cells.

311
00:22:45,280 --> 00:22:49,440
 We have a centralized core called the medulla.

312
00:22:49,440 --> 00:22:54,920
 We have the outer layer called the cortex, which surrounds the medulla, and then we have

313
00:22:54,920 --> 00:22:59,080
 the cuticle, which is the outermost layer.

314
00:22:59,080 --> 00:23:07,920
 So here we see that inner medulla, and then we have an outer layer, which is the cortex.

315
00:23:07,920 --> 00:23:14,000
 So if you follow this line, we have the outer cortex, and then surrounding the cortex will

316
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:17,880
 be the cuticle.

317
00:23:17,880 --> 00:23:25,640
 So this is just a diagram of a cross-section of a hair within its follicle.

318
00:23:25,640 --> 00:23:31,280
 We can see the follicle under the microscope.

319
00:23:31,280 --> 00:23:36,640
 So at the central portion of the hair, we have the medulla, and then surrounding the

320
00:23:36,640 --> 00:23:48,260
 medulla, the inner medulla, we have the cortex, and surrounding the cortex is the cuticle.

321
00:23:48,260 --> 00:23:52,480
 So hair follicles will extend from the epidermis into the dermis.

322
00:23:52,480 --> 00:23:55,280
 In the hair follicle, you'll have the hair bulb.

323
00:23:55,280 --> 00:24:01,120
 This is a deep, expanded end of the hair follicle, and then you have the root plexus, which is

324
00:24:01,120 --> 00:24:06,400
 a knot of sensory nerves around the hair bulb.

325
00:24:06,400 --> 00:24:11,920
 So here is a diagram of the hair bulb of the follicle.

326
00:24:11,920 --> 00:24:18,840
 So here we'll see a layer of dermal papillos, sort of like a finger-like projection there.

327
00:24:18,840 --> 00:24:24,600
 We have, again, when we saw a cross-section of it, the inner medulla, and then the outer

328
00:24:24,600 --> 00:24:28,800
 cortex, and then the cuticle of that hair follicle.

329
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:38,560
 And then we'll see maybe melanocytes at the hair bulb of the hair follicle.

330
00:24:38,560 --> 00:24:44,360
 Here again is a histone side of the hair bulb, where we can see that dermal papilla.

331
00:24:44,360 --> 00:24:46,760
 And then the innermost layer being the medulla.

332
00:24:46,760 --> 00:24:56,880
 And then around the medulla we'll see the cortex, and then the cuticle of the hair root.

333
00:24:56,880 --> 00:25:01,680
 The wall of the hair follicle is a connective tissue root sheath, and also an epithelial

334
00:25:01,680 --> 00:25:03,840
 tissue root sheath.

335
00:25:03,840 --> 00:25:06,080
 We talked about the erector pealae muscles.

336
00:25:06,080 --> 00:25:09,080
 Erector pealae muscles are a bundle of smooth muscles.

337
00:25:09,080 --> 00:25:13,680
 So if they are a smooth muscle, they are under involuntary control.

338
00:25:13,680 --> 00:25:20,000
 So hair will stand erect when the erector pealae contracts.

339
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
 So we have different types and growth of hair.

340
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:23,700
 We have villus hairs.

341
00:25:23,700 --> 00:25:26,080
 These are body hairs of women and children.

342
00:25:26,080 --> 00:25:30,960
 There are terminal hairs, which is the hair of the scalp, as well as hair of the axillary

343
00:25:30,960 --> 00:25:36,000
 and pubic area that occurs after puberty.

344
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:40,160
 And then there will be hair thinning and baldness due to aging.

345
00:25:40,160 --> 00:25:49,660
 And it is a sex-linked chromosome, so we'll get male patterned bat baldness.

346
00:25:49,660 --> 00:25:56,760
 So other accessory structures within the integumentary system, there are sebaceous glands.

347
00:25:56,760 --> 00:26:02,320
 Sebaceous glands occur over the entire body, except for the palms and soles.

348
00:26:02,320 --> 00:26:07,400
 Sebaceous glands actually secrete sebum, which is an oily substance.

349
00:26:07,400 --> 00:26:12,560
 The types of glands that secrete sebum are a simple aviolar glands.

350
00:26:12,560 --> 00:26:18,600
 So remember we talked about simple glands being just one docked and not branching.

351
00:26:18,600 --> 00:26:22,640
 Aviolar have that spherical shape to them.

352
00:26:22,640 --> 00:26:29,400
 These sebaceous glands secrete through holochrin secretion, meaning the entire cell will break

353
00:26:29,400 --> 00:26:31,800
 up to form the secretion.

354
00:26:31,800 --> 00:26:36,240
 And most sebaceous glands are associated with a hair follicle.

355
00:26:36,240 --> 00:26:39,640
 Some functions of sebum, it helps collect dirt.

356
00:26:39,640 --> 00:26:43,440
 It also softens and lubricates hair and skin.

357
00:26:43,440 --> 00:26:49,960
 So it's an oily substance that kind of helps lubricate your hair and skin.

358
00:26:49,960 --> 00:26:54,880
 So here we see the glands of the skin.

359
00:26:54,880 --> 00:26:58,280
 So we can actually see a hair within the follicle.

360
00:26:58,280 --> 00:27:13,280
 And then sort of surrounding it, we can see these sebaceous glands next to the hair follicle.

361
00:27:13,280 --> 00:27:18,920
 Other types of glands are sweat glands, which are pseudo-riferous glands that are widely

362
00:27:18,920 --> 00:27:21,040
 distributed on the body.

363
00:27:21,040 --> 00:27:23,600
 We know that sweat is actually a blood filtrate.

364
00:27:23,600 --> 00:27:29,000
 It's made up of 99% of water with some salts and contains traces of metabolic waste, so

365
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:33,920
 about 2% of urea.

366
00:27:33,920 --> 00:27:40,040
 So here we can see some sweat glands located within the dermis.

367
00:27:40,040 --> 00:27:50,480
 You can see all these cells that make up the sweat glands within the dermal layer.

368
00:27:50,480 --> 00:27:54,480
 So we have two types of sweat glands, we have an ecarin or mericrine sweat gland.

369
00:27:54,480 --> 00:27:58,880
 These are the most numerous, and these produce true sweat.

370
00:27:58,880 --> 00:28:00,920
 And then we have apocrine glands.

371
00:28:00,920 --> 00:28:06,160
 These are confined to axillary, anal, and genital areas and produce a special kind of

372
00:28:06,160 --> 00:28:07,160
 sweat.

373
00:28:07,160 --> 00:28:10,760
 It produces a musky odor, which helps attract a mate.

374
00:28:10,760 --> 00:28:18,600
 These are the pheromones that a lot of people talk about that kind of can attract another

375
00:28:18,600 --> 00:28:23,000
 person or sometimes in animals.

376
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:28,720
 They signal information about a person's immune system and contribute to the major histocompatibility

377
00:28:28,720 --> 00:28:30,360
 complex.

378
00:28:30,360 --> 00:28:34,240
 And then we have serum minus glands and mammary glands.

379
00:28:34,240 --> 00:28:41,440
 These are also modified apocrine glands.

380
00:28:41,440 --> 00:28:45,880
 So when we talk about skin, we want to talk about common emergencies associated with skin.

381
00:28:45,880 --> 00:28:47,840
 We want to talk about burns.

382
00:28:47,840 --> 00:28:55,480
 So burns are caused by heat, chemicals, explosions, electricity, and solar radiations.

383
00:28:55,480 --> 00:28:59,280
 Critical burns can be life threatening, and quite immediate medical attention can cause

384
00:28:59,280 --> 00:29:04,600
 breathing difficulty, especially if it covers more than one body part.

385
00:29:04,600 --> 00:29:09,920
 Burns are emergencies when they affect the head, neck, hands, feet, and genitals, and

386
00:29:09,920 --> 00:29:13,680
 also their emergencies if there are burns to a child or older adult.

387
00:29:13,680 --> 00:29:19,920
 So either they're really young or they're really old other than minor burns.

388
00:29:19,920 --> 00:29:23,600
 Burns are classified by severity.

389
00:29:23,600 --> 00:29:28,480
 First degree burn occurs when only the upper epidermis is damaged.

390
00:29:28,480 --> 00:29:30,760
 So it's not as severe.

391
00:29:30,760 --> 00:29:36,520
 Second degree burns when you have the upper part of the dermis as well as the epidermis

392
00:29:36,520 --> 00:29:41,200
 is also damaged and you can have blistering or blisters that appear.

393
00:29:41,200 --> 00:29:44,000
 But the skin can heal with very little scarring.

394
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:49,520
 However, third degree burns, this will consume the entire thickness of the skin, meaning all

395
00:29:49,520 --> 00:29:51,160
 the layers.

396
00:29:51,160 --> 00:29:57,200
 Burned areas may appear white, red, or blackened, and you can either have great pain if the nerve

397
00:29:57,200 --> 00:30:03,720
 endings are destroyed, or it could be painless, sorry, great pain, or if the nerve endings

398
00:30:03,720 --> 00:30:08,640
 are destroyed, the burn may be painless.

399
00:30:08,640 --> 00:30:14,760
 So here we actually can see the different types of burns.

400
00:30:14,760 --> 00:30:18,760
 First degree burn again, only occurring within the epidermis.

401
00:30:18,760 --> 00:30:23,240
 The second degree burn affecting both the dermis and the epidermis.

402
00:30:23,240 --> 00:30:29,800
 And then third degree burns occurring throughout the full thickness of the skin.

403
00:30:29,800 --> 00:30:36,640
 In order to estimate the extent of burns, we use what's called the rule of nines.

404
00:30:36,640 --> 00:30:44,320
 We associate a percentage to a specific body part, for example, the front part of the upper

405
00:30:44,320 --> 00:30:53,000
 limb accounts for 4.5%, whereas the anterior trunk accounts for 18%.

406
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:58,960
 And we add this up to kind of figure out the percentage of the body that has been burned.

407
00:30:58,960 --> 00:31:03,920
 So this is called the rule of nines.

408
00:31:03,920 --> 00:31:06,960
 When we talk about skin, we also want to talk about skin cancer, and there are different

409
00:31:06,960 --> 00:31:08,720
 types of skin cancer.

410
00:31:08,720 --> 00:31:13,160
 First type of skin cancer is basal cell carcinoma.

411
00:31:13,160 --> 00:31:17,000
 Basal cell carcinoma is the least malignant and the most common.

412
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:22,640
 So again, it will affect the basal cell layers within the epidermis.

413
00:31:22,640 --> 00:31:30,880
 Squamous cell carcinoma arises from keratinocytes of the stratum spinosum, and then we have melanoma.

414
00:31:30,880 --> 00:31:36,560
 Melanoma is a cancer of the melanocytes, and it is the most dangerous type of skin cancer.

415
00:31:36,560 --> 00:31:44,640
 It has a high rate of metastasis, meaning it spreads to other organs and can have a very

416
00:31:44,640 --> 00:31:47,920
 poor prognosis.

417
00:31:47,920 --> 00:31:51,680
 So here we see an example of basal cell carcinoma.

418
00:31:51,680 --> 00:31:59,360
 Here is a squamous cell carcinoma, and here is a dark mole or a dark lesion indicative

419
00:31:59,360 --> 00:32:02,360
 of melanoma.

420
00:32:02,360 --> 00:32:07,640
 So that is why when we have patients, we want to make sure, especially if they are prone

421
00:32:07,640 --> 00:32:12,240
 to skin cancer, we want to check if they have any moles.

422
00:32:12,240 --> 00:32:16,400
 We also want to check our ABCs when it comes to moles.

423
00:32:16,400 --> 00:32:18,120
 So A, we want to check for appearance.

424
00:32:18,120 --> 00:32:23,680
 If there are any changes in appearance, B, we want to look for borders, whether or not

425
00:32:23,680 --> 00:32:27,920
 they have distinctive borders or irregular borders.

426
00:32:27,920 --> 00:32:32,520
 C, we want to check for color, and whether or not there are darker color or reddish color

427
00:32:32,520 --> 00:32:35,160
 or if there is any change in color.

428
00:32:35,160 --> 00:32:43,320
 And D, we want to check for diameter, whether or not the mole has gotten bigger or has increased

429
00:32:43,320 --> 00:32:44,320
 in diameter.

430
00:32:44,320 --> 00:32:53,840
 So check your ABCDs for moles to kind of do a quick check for melanoma or skin cancer.

431
00:32:53,840 --> 00:33:01,360
 So we know that the epidermal layer will develop from embryonic ectoderm, however the dermis

432
00:33:01,360 --> 00:33:04,640
 and hypodermis will develop from mesoderm.

433
00:33:04,640 --> 00:33:11,520
 Milano sites develop from neural crest cells within the fetus.

434
00:33:11,520 --> 00:33:16,720
 The fetal skin is well formed after the fourth month, and then after or at five to six months,

435
00:33:16,720 --> 00:33:21,800
 the fetus is covered in downy hairs called lanugo.

436
00:33:21,800 --> 00:33:26,680
 We have fetal sebaceous glands that produce the vernix casioso.

437
00:33:26,680 --> 00:33:37,880
 So sort of this cheesy thick layer that you'll see once the baby has been born.

438
00:33:37,880 --> 00:33:45,000
 Throughout life, middle to old age, the skin will thin out and become less elastic, meaning

439
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:51,760
 the fibers that are usually predominant within our skin are no longer being produced as

440
00:33:51,760 --> 00:33:54,380
 much as we get older.

441
00:33:54,380 --> 00:33:59,640
 Also skin can start showing the harmful effects of environmental damage, especially for those

442
00:33:59,640 --> 00:34:06,760
 who are sun worshipers, who didn't wear sunscreen when they were younger, who got forbid, you

443
00:34:06,760 --> 00:34:09,600
 know, went into those tanning beds.

444
00:34:09,600 --> 00:34:12,560
 I'm probably guilty of them when I was a teenager.

445
00:34:12,560 --> 00:34:18,400
 That was the cool thing to do to sit in tanning beds before a dance or before prom.

446
00:34:18,400 --> 00:34:24,160
 And I do regret it because those are basically cancer beds.

447
00:34:24,160 --> 00:34:31,360
 They give a very high dose of UV radiation to your skin, and you can definitely, you

448
00:34:31,360 --> 00:34:34,520
 know, after prolonged usage, could cause cancer.

449
00:34:34,520 --> 00:34:40,000
 Also, you know, if you expose yourself to that much sun and UV radiation, you start getting

450
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:42,920
 wrinkles and you start looking like you're aging early.

451
00:34:42,920 --> 00:34:47,600
 So if you can, try to avoid the sun between the hours of ten and two.

452
00:34:47,600 --> 00:34:52,240
 And if you have to be out, make sure you're wearing a hat or some sort of protective gear

453
00:34:52,240 --> 00:34:56,600
 to protect your skin, because honestly, once you get old, you know, you can't stop the

454
00:34:56,600 --> 00:34:57,600
 wrinkling process.

455
00:34:57,600 --> 00:34:59,240
 You can't stop the aging process.

456
00:34:59,240 --> 00:35:03,640
 So while you're young, take care of your skin now.

457
00:35:03,640 --> 00:35:08,200
 Also when you get older, skin inflammations do become more common.

458
00:35:08,200 --> 00:35:09,200
 Okay, guys.

459
